1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yu Liang 庾亮 (289–340), ''zi'' Yuangui 元規. Eastern Jin statesman and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yu Liang's ancestral home was Yanling 鄢陵 in Yingchuan 潁川 commandery (northwest of modern Yanling, Henan). His father Yu Chen 庾琛 (d. 316) was a prominent military official at the end of the Western Jin. In 311, his younger sister Y
2 CONC u Wenjun 庾文君 (297–328) married Sima Shao 司馬紹 (299–325), the future Emperor Ming (r. 322–325). She was named empress in July 323. After Sima Shao died in 325, she was de facto ruler for the young Emperor Cheng (r. 325–342). Her inf
2 CONC luence at the court was an important factor in Yu Liang's success early in his official career. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yu Liang was handsome and an eloquent speaker. He also had a strong interest in the study of the ''Laozi'' and ''Zhuangzi''. In 304, he was offered an appointment on the staff of the Prince of Donghai, Sima Yue 司馬越 (d. 311), but h
2 CONC e did not take up the post. In 311, Yu Liang accompanied his father to Guiji who was assigned there as governor. During this time Sima Rui 司馬睿 (276–322), the future founder of the Eastern Jin, appointed Yu Liang an assistant in th
2 CONC e western section of his headquarters. It was also at this time that Yu Liang's sister became a concubine of Sima Rui's eldest son Sima Shao. Yu Liang soon became an aide on Sima Rui' staff. He also was given the noble title of Ma
2 CONC rquis of Fengdu neighborhood 封都亭. In 313, Yu Liang was put in charge of military affairs under Sima Rui. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the founding of the Eastern Jin in 317, Yu Liang was appointed director of the Secretariat. He also was put in charge of the editorial service in the palace library and was assigned as reader-in-waiting to the crown prince Si
2 CONC ma Shao. Within a few years, Yu Liang was named supervising secretary, followed by an appointment as gentleman attendant at the palace gate. In February 322, Wang Dun 王敦 (266–324) rebelled at Wuchang. Sima Rui sent Yu Liang to con
2 CONC sult Wang Dun at the strategic town of Wuhu 蕪湖 (modern Wuhu, Anhui). Wang Dun was so impressed with Yu, he declared that Yu was superior to Pei Wei 裴頠 (267–300). Sima Rui appointed Yu Liang capital commandant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Sima Shao ascended the imperial throne in January 323, he named Yu Liang secretariat supervisor to replace Wang Dao 王導 (276–339). Yu Liang presented a long petition to the court declining the post. The emperor withdrew the ap
2 CONC pointment. However, upon learning of this, Wang Dun became suspicious of Yu Liang. Fearing Wang Dun, Yu Liang resigned office on grounds of illness. In 324, Wang Dun revolted again. Yu Liang was appointed left general of the guard
2 CONC s and helped lead the armies that resisted the attack on the capital led by Wang Dun's general Qian Feng 錢 鳳 (d. 324). As a reward for his service Yu Liang was given the title Duke of Yongchang. He declined the title. He was named
2 CONC  protector-general instead. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In late September, early October 325, Emperor Ming fell seriously ill. Yu Liang was one of seven officials who were summoned to receive the emperor's testamentary edict. Yu Liang was named supervising secretary and secretariat dir
2 CONC ector. When Emperor Cheng took the throne on 5 October 325, Yu Liang was designated co-regent with Wang Dao. However, Yu Liang had much more authority than Wang at this time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 327, Su Jun 蘇峻 (d. 328) rose in revolt from his base in Liyang 歷陽 (modern He 和 county, Anhui). In March 328, he invaded Jiankang and took the young Emperor Cheng prisoner. Loyalists led by Yu Liang mounted a counter-at
2 CONC tack, but when Su Jun's army overran Jiankang, Yu Liang fled to Xunyang 尋陽 (modern Jiujiang, Jiangxi). Yu Liang was one of several military commanders who defeated Su Jun's forces at Jiankang in October 328. After the young empero
2 CONC r had returned to the imperial palace, Yu Liang presented him with a petition requesting to retire to the countryside, blaming himself for Su Jun's rebellion. Yu Liang planned “to flee to the mountains and seas.” As he was about t
2 CONC o depart from Jiyang 曁陽 (east of modern Jiangyin 江陰, Jiangsu), the emperor ordered the authorities to seize his boat to prevent him from leaving. Yu Liang requested to be assigned to a provincial post. In 329 he was appointed regi
2 CONC onal inspector of Yuzhou 豫州 (administrative seat, Wuhu 蕪湖, Anhui) and administrator of Xuancheng 宣城 (modern Xuanzhou 宣州, Anhui). In 330, Yu Liang declined the titles of Commander-in-chief of Expeditionary Forces and General Who De
2 CONC fends the West. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 334, Tao Kan 陶侃 (259–334), who had held the position of regional inspector of Xiangzhou 湘州, Guangzhou 廣州, and Jingzhou 荊州 (modern Hunan, Hubei, Guangdong, and Guangxi), died. Yu Liang was put in charge of military affairs in th
2 CONC e six provinces of Jiangzhou 江州, Jingzhou, Yuzhou, Yizhou 益州, Liangzhou 梁州, and Yongzhou 雍州 (modern Jiangxi, Hubei, Anhui, southern Shaanxi, and Sichuan), and was assigned as regional inspector of Jiangzhou, Jingzhou, and Yuzhou. 
2 CONC His headquarters was at Wuchang 武昌 (modern Wuchang, Hubei). In 338, Yu Liang sent a letter to Defender-in-chief Xi Jian 郗鑒 (269–339) recommending that Wang Dao be removed from his position as regent. Before his death, Tao Kan had 
2 CONC requested to be allowed to lead an army to remove Wang Dao. Xi Jian had refused to allow this action. Although Yu Liang's prose was quite compelling, Xi Jian denied his request. 
2 CONT
2 CONT About this same time, Yu Liang proposed to organize an army to recover the north, which had become unstable after the death of Shi Le 石勒 in 333. Yu Liang assigned various men under his command to station armies at Zhucheng 邾城 (nor
2 CONC thwest of modern Huanggang 黃岡, Hubei), Mian 沔 (the Han River at modern Hankou), and Jiangling. He had another commander assigned as regional inspector of Liangzhou and led a force toward the Ziwu 子午 Valley that was the entryway to
2 CONC  Chang'an. Yu Liang led an army of 100,000 men to Shicheng 石城 at Xiangyang. He sent a petition to the imperial court requesting permission to launch an expedition to the north. Although Wang Dao concurred, Xi Jian objected on the 
2 CONC grounds that the forces were lacking in sufficient provisions. Yu Liang petitioned the court to be allowed to move his headquarters elsewhere. Just at that time a force led by the Later Zhao general Zhang Hedu 張賀度 overan Zhucheng.
2 CONC  Yu Liang made apologies to the emperor, and reduced his own rank three degrees. The emperor issued an edict restoring his position. He also appointed him minister of works, but Yu declined the position. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon thereafter Wang Dao died. Emperor Cheng named Yu Liang minister of education and regional inspector of Yangzhou 揚州 (administrative seat, modern Nanjing). Yu Liang had become seriously ill as a result of the failure of his nor
2 CONC thern expedition. He adamantly refused these appointments. The emperor approved his request to leave office. Yu Liang died in 340 at the age of fifty-two. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Yu Liang's collected writings in twenty-one ''juan'' and mentions a Liang catalogue that recorded his collection as consisting of twenty ''juan'' plus a table of contents in o
2 CONC ne ''juan''. The extra ''juan'' in the Sui listing very likely included the table of contents. Both Tang histories record the collection in twenty ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Yan Kejun has collected twenty-one of Yu Liang
2 CONC 's prose writings in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qian Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''. He is best known for his petitions. His “Rang zhongshu jian biao” was selected for the ''Wen xuan'' and is often praised as a model example of the ''biao'' 
2 CONC 表 or “expression of personal sentiment” type of petition. Another moving petition is his “Shangshu qi haigu” 上疏乞骸骨 (Petition submitted begging to have my bones returned home) in which he requested to be allowed to retire from offi
2 CONC ce. In one section he said of himself: “I have betrayed the state, and my crime is so great, it is something that even heaven cannot enfold, or the earth can bear.” In another place he wrote: “From antiquity to the present has the
2 CONC re ever been a worst case of disloyalty and unfiliality than mine?” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhong Rong lists Yu Liang in the lower grade of the ''Shi pin'', but none of his poems has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Zhang Guo'an 張國安. “Yu Liang zhuanquan yu Dong Jin tongzhi jituan de neibu douzheng” 庾亮專權與東晉統治集團的內部鬪爭. Henan shifan daxue xuebao (1988: 2); 40–44, 73. 
2 CONT * Meng Fanye 孟繁冶. “Han Wei Jin Nanchao shiqi Yingchuan Yanling Yushi jiazu yuanliu kao” 漢魏晉南朝時期潁川鄢陵庾氏家族源流考. Xuchang shizhuan xuebao (Shehui kexue ban) 11.3 (1992): 97–102. 
2 CONT * Meng Fanye 孟繁冶. “Lun Yingchuan Yanling Yushi zhi jueqi” 論潁川鄢陵庾氏之崛起. Xuchang shizhuan xuebao (Shehui kexue ban) 12.1 (1993): 100–12; 12.3 (1993): 85–89. 
2 CONT * Xu Gongchi 徐公持. Wei Jin wenxue shi 魏晉文學史, 464–67. Beijing: Renmin wenxue chubanshe, 1999. 
2 CONT * Li Jicang 李濟滄. “Lun Yu Liang” 論庾亮. Zhonghua wenshi luncong (2006: 3): 179–212. 
2 CONT * Zhao Yan 趙燕. “Yingchuan Yushi wenxue chuangzuo zhong de xuanfo sixiang” 潁川庾氏文學創作中的玄佛思想. Pingdianshan xueyuan xuebao 22.1 (2007): 40–42. 
2 CONT * Zhou Weiming 周偉明. “Dong Jin Yingchuan Yushi jiazu yanjiu” 東晉潁川庾氏家族研究. Wenshi bolan (Lilun) (December 2009): 27–29.
2 CONT
