1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yu You 庾友 (fl. 353–384), ''zi'' Yutai 玉臺. Eastern Jin poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yu You's ancestral home was Yanling 鄢陵 in Yingchuan 穎川 (northwest of  modern Yanling, Henan). He was the third son of Yu Bing 庾冰 (296–344), who was a younger brother of the celebrated writer Yu Liang 庾亮 (289– 340). Little is known
2 CONC  about his life. According to Yu Bing's biography in the ''Jin shu'', the Yu brothers attained success in their official careers because of their connections with the imperial family. Yu You's sister was married to Sima Yi 司馬奕 (34
2 CONC 2–386), Emperor Fei (r. 365–371). During his reign, Yu You was appointed governor of Dongyang 東陽 (southeast of modern Xuyi 盱眙, Jiangsu), but he lived in Jiyang 曁陽 (east of modern Jiangyin 江陰, Jiangsu). One of his brothers Yu Xi 庾希
2 CONC  (n.d.) resided in Shanyang 山陽 (modern Huai'an 淮安, Jiangsu). These three places all have the character “yang.” It was said that Guo Pu 郭璞 (276–324), the celebrated late Western and early Eastern Jin writer, predicted Yu Bing's fut
2 CONC ure by saying that “your children and grandchildren will encounter some major disasters. The only way you can have descendants is to have three yang 陽 in your life.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 371, Huan Wen 桓溫 (312–373) deposed Emperor Fei. He executed two of Yu You's younger brothers, Yu Qian 庾倩 (n.d.) and Yu Rou 庾柔 (n.d.). Yu You's eldest brother Yu Xi 庾希 fled to a swamp in Hailing 海陵 (modern Taizhou 泰州, Jiangsu) w
2 CONC ith his younger brother Yu Miao 庾邈 (n.d.) and his son Yu Youzhi 庾攸之 (n.d.). However, they later were all killed by Huan Wen. When You Yu was just about to be executed, his daughter-in-law, who was Huan Wen's niece, begged Huan Wen
2 CONC  to pardon her father-in-law. Huan Wen decided to spare Yu You's life. Among the seven Yu brothers, only Yu You and Yu Yun 庾蘊 were not executed. Yu Yun later drank poisoned wine and died in Guangzhou 廣州. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 353, Yu You and Yu Yun and twenty-four other men attended the gathering at Lan ting 蘭亭 hosted by Wang Xizhi 王羲之 (303–361). Yu You's only extant work is the poem he composed for this occasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 411.
2 CONT
2 CONT TPK
