1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yu Yun 庾蘊 (fl. 366–384). Jin poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yu Yun's ancestral home was Yanling 鄢陵 in Yingchuan 穎川 (northwest of modern Yanling, Henan). He was the fourth son of Yu Bing 庾冰 (296– 344), who was a younger brother of the celebrated writer Yu Liang 庾亮 (289–340). Little is known
2 CONC  about his life. According to Yu Bing's biography in the Jin shu, the Yu brothers attained success in their official careers because of their connections with the imperial family. Their sister married Sima Yi 司馬奕 (342–386), Empero
2 CONC r Fei (r. 365–371). During the Taihe period (366–371), Yu Yun was appointed regional inspector of Guangzhou 廣州 (modern Guangdong and Guangxi areas). In 371, Huan Wen 桓溫 (312–373) deposed Emperor Fei and executed most of the Yu bro
2 CONC thers. According to the Jin shu, Yu Yun drank poison and died in Guangzhou. However, the Yungu zaji 雲谷雜記 by Zhang Hao 張淏 (fl. 1216) mentions that in 383 Yu Yun attended the Lan ting 蘭亭 gathering in Guiji at which he composed a pen
2 CONC tasyllablic poem. Thus, Yu Yun may not have committed suicide, but returned to Guiji where he resided with his brother Yu You 庾友 (fl. 353–384). Furthermore, the names and titles of all participants at the Lan ting gathering are re
2 CONC corded in the Yungu zaji. Those who no longer served in office are identified with the word “former” preceding their most recent official title. Yu Yun and his brother Yu You do not have any titles attached to their names. This ma
2 CONC y indicate that they were accused criminals and had lost their official status. Lu Qinli has collected Yu Yun's only extant poem, “Lan ting shi” 蘭亭詩 (Poem on Lan ting gathering), in his Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbeichao shi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 417–18.
2 CONT
2 CONT TPK
