1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wang Min 王珉 (351–388), ''zi'' Jiyan 季琰. Eastern Jin calligrapher and poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Min's ancestral home was Linyi 臨沂 in Langye 琅邪 commandery (modern Linyi, Shandong). His grandfather was the statesman Wang Dao 王導 (276–339). His father was Wang Dao's third and most distinguished son Wang Qia 王洽 (323–358). He
2 CONC  is the younger brother of Wang Xun 王珣 (349–400). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 368, Wang Min was invited to serve as recorder in his local area, but he declined the appointment. About this same time he married a daughter of Xie An 謝安 (320–385). He later served as editorial director in the palace library, 
2 CONC gentleman cavalier attendant, professor in the national university, gentleman attendant at the palace gate, and palace gentleman. In 386, when Wang Xianzhi 王獻之 (344–386) died, Wang Min replaced him as ''zhongshu ling'' 中書令 (secret
2 CONC ariat director). Wang Xianzhi was thereafter known as ''da ling'' 大令 (senior director), and Wang Min was referred to as ''xiao ling'' 小令 (junior director). Wang Min died in 388 at the age of thirty-eight. He was posthumously grant
2 CONC ed the position of chamberlain for ceremonials. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Wang Min's collected works in ten ''juan''. This was lost already in the Tang.
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 王珉 Wang Min, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/COM-0184
