1 NAME 湛 孝若 /沛國譙縣/
2 GIVN 湛 孝若
2 SURN 沛國譙縣
2 _MARNM 夏侯
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 243
1 DEAT
2 DATE 291
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xiahou Zhan 夏侯湛 (243–291), ''zi'' Xiaoruo 孝若. Late Wei, early Western Jin writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiahou Zhan's ancestral home was Qiao 譙 (modern Bozhou 亳州, Anhui, Anhui). His great-grandfather was Xiahou Yuan 夏侯淵 (d. 219), one of Cao Cao's most able military men who was married to one of Cao Cao's sisters. in 262, at age twen
2 CONC ty, Xiahou Zhan began his career as clerk on the staff of Grand Commandant Gao Rou 高柔 (174–263). He continued to serve a succession of grand commandants until 269 when he was recommended along with Zhi Yu 摯虞 (d. 311) to the imperi
2 CONC al court as “worthy and good.” This designation qualified him to reply to the examination questions that were posed in this year. Xiahou Zhan's essay earned him an appointment as gentleman of the palace, a 300-bushel rank. It was 
2 CONC at this time he met Pan Yue 潘岳 (247–300), who had just been appointed clerk on the staff of the minister of works. These two handsome young men were referred to as “linked jade discs.” Xiahou Zhan's material grandmother Xin Xianyi
2 CONC ng 辛憲英 (191–269) died in 269, and Zhan wrote a biography of her. Xin Xianying's husband Yang Dan 羊耽 was the uncle of the famous minister and general Yang Hu 羊祜 (221–278) who helped plan the conquest of Wu. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his distinguished family background Xiahou Zhan was disappointed that he held low office for such a long period. Thus, in 273, he composed “Di yi” 抵疑 (rebutting suspicions) in which he angrily vents his frustration that hi
2 CONC s official career has been frustrated by his failure to obtain recognition at court. At various places in the piece Xiahou Zhan even intimates that he will leave the court and go into reclusion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ca. 274, Xiahou Zhan received appointment as secretary on the staff of the heir designate Sima Zhong 司馬衷 (259–306), the future Emperor Hui 惠帝 (r. 291–306). In 277, upon the recommendation of Shan Tao 山濤 (205–283) he was transferre
2 CONC d to the post of secretarial court gentleman. Ca. 279–280, Xiahou Zhan joined the force led by Jia Chong 賈充 (217– 282) who directed the conquest of Wu in 280. Presumably upon his return from Wu (280), Xiahou Zhan was appointed mag
2 CONC istrate of Yewang 野王 (modern Qinyang 沁陽, Henan). His administrative duties here were light, and he had time to compose “Kundi gao” 昆弟誥 (Pronouncements for my brothers), a long essay written in the style of the ''gao'' 誥 (pronounce
2 CONC ments) of the ''Shang shu'' in which he lectures his six brothers on benevolence, propriety, and cultivation of character. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiahou Zhan remained in Yewang for five years. In 285, he was summoned to the imperial court where took up the post of vice-director of the secretariat. He served in this position until 287 when he was assigned as administrator on
2 CONC  the staff of the prince of Nanyang 南陽 (modern Nanyang, Henan), Sima Jian 司馬柬 (262–291). Nanyang was the home of the famous Eastern Han scholar and writer Zhang Heng. Xiahou Zhan wrote a stele inscription in Zhang Heng's honor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 290, Xiahou Zhan was summoned to the capital to serve as coachman to the heir designate, Sima Zhong. However, he was unable to take up the post, for Emperor Wu died, and Sima Zhong succeeded him as emperor. Xiahou Zhan was then
2 CONC  given the prestigious position of cavalier attendant-in-ordinary. Xiahou Zhan died in 291 at the age of forty-nine ''sui''. Pan Yue composed his dirge which is included in the ''Wen xuan''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiahou Zhan had a collection in ten ''juan'' that survived as late as the Northern Song. A collection of his essays, the ''Xin lun'' 新論 (New disquisitions) in ten ''juan'', also was extant in the Northern Song. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiahou Zhan was a prolific ''fu'' writer. Yan Kejun collected fragments of twenty-five ''fu'' a number of which are on ''yong wu'' subjects such as pomegranate (''anshi liu'' 安石榴), orange daylily (''yinan hua'' 宜男花), lotus (''furo
2 CONC ng'' 芙蓉), shepherd's purse (''ji'' 薺), and duckweed (''fuping'' 浮萍). He also wrote several pieces on atmospheric phenomena such as snow, lightning, and thunder. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Among Xiahou Zhan's most distinctive pieces are nine poems in “Chu song” meter. They include two pieces he wrote in 279–280 when he participated in the military campaign against Wu: “Li qin yong” 離親詠 (Song of separation from kinsm
2 CONC en) and “Jiangshang fan ge” 江上泛歌 (Song of drifting on the Yangzi). He also has a group of pieces on feelings associated with seasons: “Chun kele” 春可樂 (Spring is delightful), “Qiu keai” 秋可哀 (Autumn is lamentable), “Qiu xi ai” 秋夕哀 (
2 CONC Lament on an autumn night), “Han ku yao” 寒苦謠 (Song of the miseries of winter cold). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiahou Zhan did not write any pentasyllabic verse. However, he composed “Zhou shi” 周詩 (Zhou poems), a set of tetrasyllabic poems to supply lyrics for the six ''Shi jing'' poems that were transmitted with titles but no lyrics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiahou Zhan was a prolific prose writer. He was especially noted for his writings about people. His collection contains ten ''zan'' 贊 (encomia) on historical figures such as Yan Hui, Guan Zhong, Lu Zhonglian, and Zhuangzi. The onl
2 CONC y complete ''zan'' is “Dongfang Shuo hua zan” 東方朔畫贊  (Encomium for the portrait of Dongfang Shuo), which is contained in the ''Wen xuan''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiahou Zhan also wrote a set of biographies of family members. Most of these are titled ''xu'' 敘 (accounts) and include biographies of Xiahou Cheng 夏侯稱 and Xiahou Rong 夏侯榮, two sons of Xiahou Yuan who died young, Yang Hu's eldest 
2 CONC son Yang Bing 羊秉, and his maternal grandmother Xin Xianying mentioned above.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Collections
2 CONT
2 CONT * Zhang Xie 張燮 (1574–1640), ed. Xiahou changshi ji 夏侯常侍集. 2 juan. Qishi'er jia ji 七十二家集. rpt. Xuxiu Siku quanshu, v. 1584. 
2 CONT * Zhang Pu 張溥 (1602–1641), ed. Xiahou changshi ji 夏侯常侍集. 1 juan. Han Wei Liuchao baisan mingjia ji. 
2 CONT * Xin lun 新論 (new disquisitions)
2 CONT
