1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Hun 謝混 (381?–412), ''zi'' Shuyuan 叔源, childhood name Yishou 益壽. Eastern Jin and Liu-Song period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Hun's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen commandery 陳郡 (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He was a grandson of Xie An 謝安 (320– 385) and the second cousin of Xie Lingyun. His father Xie Yan 謝琰 (d. 400) was given the noble title Duk
2 CONC e of Wangcai for his service in the battle at the Fei River. Xie Yan was killed when the army of Sun En 孫恩 (d. 402) invaded Guiji 會稽 (modern Shaoxing area) in 400. Xie Hun inherited his father's title. Xie Hun's wife was the Princ
2 CONC ess of Jinling 晉陵 (d. 432), the daughter of Emperor Xiaowu (r. 373–396) of the Eastern Jin. Xie Hun held high office in the late Eastern Jin, including secretariat director (405) and capital commandant (406). It was about this tim
2 CONC e that he threw his support to Liu Yi 劉毅 (d. 412) who was a rival of the eventual Song dynasty founder Liu Yu 劉裕 (356–422). When Wang Mi 王謐 (360–407), regional inspector of Yangzhou 揚州 died in 407, Xie Hun was recommended to succe
2 CONC ed him. However, Liu Yu took the position himself. In November 412, Liu Yu led his armies against Liu Yi, and Xie Hun was taken prisoner. He was “granted permission to commit suicide.” Xie Hun's last position was that of ''shangsh
2 CONC u zuo puye'' 尚書左僕射 (vice-director of the secretariat), and thus he is often referred as Vice-director Xie. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Hun was the leading figure of a Xie family literary group that included Xie Lingyun, Xie Hongwei 謝弘微 (392–433), Xie Zhan 謝瞻 (383?–421), and Xie Yao 謝曜 (d. 427). They gathered periodically in the family home located in the Wuyi
2 CONC  xiang 烏衣巷 district of the capital Jiankang. This group was called the “Wuyi associates” 烏衣之遊. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Hun's collected works consisted of five ''juan'' in Liang times, but was reduced to three ''juan'' in the early Tang. The collection was lost by the end of the Tang. He is also the compiler of a large sixty-''juan'' anthology,
2 CONC  ''Ji yuan'' 集苑 that survived until the Northern Song. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the early medieval period, Xie Hun was regarded as a poet who led the transition from ''xuanyan'' poetry to landscape verse. However, only two complete pieces, “You Xichi” 遊西池 (Roaming Western Pond) and “Jie zu zi shi” 誡族子詩
2 CONC  (Poem of warning to cousins), are preserved. There are also fragments of three other pieces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Chang Pei-pei 張蓓蓓. “Dong Jin shijia Sun Xu Yin Xie tongkao” 東晉詩家孫許殷謝通考. Guoli Taiwan daxue wen shi zhe xuebao 46 (1997): 295–324; rpt. in Zhang Beibei. Wei Jin xueshu renwu xin yanjiu 魏晉學術人物新研究, 231–83. Taipei: Da'an chubanshe, 
2 CONC 2001. 
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. “Fengshen xiuche de Xie Hun he shanshui shi” 風神秀徹的謝混山水詩. Dong Jin Nanchao de Xieshi wenxue jituan 東晉南朝的謝氏文學集團, 46–57. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998. 
2 CONT * Li Yan 李雁. “ ‘Wuyi zhi you' kao shu” “烏衣之遊”考述. Shandong jiaoyu xueyuan xuebao 81 (2000): 20–25.
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. “Xie Hun he tada shanshui shi” 謝混和他的山水詩. Gudian wenxue zhishi (2004: 4): 63–69. 
2 CONT * Xu Mingying 徐明英 and Gu Nong 顧農. “Lun Xie Hun zai Jin Song shifeng bian-qianzhong de zuoyong” 論謝混在晉宋詩風變遷中的作用. Xihua daxue xuebao (Zhexue shehui kexue ban) 3 (2006): 27–30. 
2 CONT * Hu Dalei 胡大雷. “Yin Zhongwen Xie Hun dui xuanyan shi de zhongjie” 殷仲文謝混對玄言詩的終結. Hechi xueyuan xuebao (2006: 1): 51–54; rpt. Hu Dalei. Xuanyan shi yanjiu, 259–70.
2 CONT
