2 DATE 415
1 DEAT
2 DATE 453
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wang Wei 王微 (415–453), ''zi'' Jingxuan 景玄. Liu-Song period calligrapher, painter, and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Wei's ancestral home was Linyi 臨沂, Langye 琅邪 commandery (modern Linyi, Shandong). He was the son of Wang Ru 王儒, a younger brother of Wang Hong 王弘 (379–432), who was one of the most distinguished court officials at the beginni
2 CONC ng of the Song dynasty. During Wang Wei's youth he was a hardworking student, and he acquired expertise in music, medicine, and the occult arts. Ca. 431 he began his official career on the staff of the Prince of Pengcheng 彭城, Liu 
2 CONC Yikang 劉義康 (409–451). Some time later he joined the entourage of Liu Jun 劉濬 (429–453), Prince of Shixing 始興. He also served as secretary to the heir designate. When Wang Wei's father died, he resigned from office to observe mourni
2 CONC ng for him. After the mourning period, he returned to official service as an aide to Liu Shuo 劉鑠 (431–453), Prince of Nanping 南平. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he served in various positions in his youth, Wang Wei had little interest in pursuing an official career. Thus, he declined many invitations to take office. Ca. 452, Director of the Ministry of Personnel Jiang Zhan 江湛 (40
2 CONC 8–453) recommended Wang Wei for the position of vice director of the Ministry of Personnel. Wang Wei sent Jiang a letter refusing the invitation. His letter is interesting, for it is written in imitation of the famous letter Xi Ka
2 CONC ng 嵇康 (223–263) sent to Shan Tao 山濤 (205–283), in which Xi Kang rebukes Shan Tao and breaks off friendship with him for recommending him for a position at the court. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Wei led a simple life, and spent much of his time collecting books and ancient artifacts. In 453, Wang Wei's younger brother Wang Sengqian 王僧謙 (414–453) died of illness. Wang Wei blamed himself for giving him an ineffective h
2 CONC erbal medicine, and he died forty days after his brother at the age of thirty-nine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Wei preferred to write in cadenced ancient-style prose rather than the parallel prose form that was then in vogue. Yuan Shu 袁淑 (408–453), whose father's older sister was married to Wang Wei's uncle Wang Hong, remarked that Wa
2 CONC ng Wei's writings were “complaints voicing grievances.” Wang then wrote a letter to his younger cousin Wang Sengchuo 王僧綽 (423–453) in which he replied that “if writing does not contain complaint and criticism, it is bland and insi
2 CONC pid.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhong Rong ranked Wang Wei's poetry in the middle grade of his ''Shi pin''. The monographs on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' and the two Tang histories list his collected works in ten ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Zhong Ro
2 CONC ng mentions the ''Hong bao'' 鴻寶 (Vast treasure), which may be the same as a ten-''juan'' work of the same title with no named author listed in the monograph on bibliography in the ''Sui shu''. This is no longer extant. It seems to
2 CONC  have been a set of notes on literature. Yan Kejun has collected ten of Wang Wei's prose works in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen'', and Lu Qinli has included his five extant poems in ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbei
2 CONC chao shi''. The ''Wen xuan'' contains one of his poems titled “Za shi” 雜詩 (Unclassified poem). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Wei's best known prose work is “Xu hua” 敘畫 (Explaining painting). The received version is probably not complete. It actually is a reply to a letter Wang Wei had received from Yan Yanzhi 顏延之 (384–456), who had stated that pain
2 CONC ting is more than just the work of an artisan, but belongs to the same realm as the “images” (''xiang'' 象) of the Classic of Changes. Wang Wei devotes his essay to explaining how painting is not limited to drawing maps or pictures
2 CONC  of landforms and waterways, but involves the fusing of the “numinous” (''ling'' 靈) and “form” (''xing'' 形). He proceeds to mention many of the subjects of landscape painting, especially famous mountains. In the final paragraph Wa
2 CONC ng claims that painting natural scenes ultimately involves a “descent of the spirit” into the painting and perhaps even the painting itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Acker, William r. B. Some T'ang and Pre-T'ang Texts on Chinese Painting, Volume Two, Part One, 129–37. Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1974. 
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 37–38. 
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and shen Yucheng, Zhonggu wenxue shiliao congkao, 321–22. 
2 CONT * Zhang Yibin 張義賓. “Zong Bing, Wang Wei meixue sixiang de benti yiyi” 宗炳、王微美學思想的本體意義. Dongnan daxue xuebao 7.5 (2005): 51–54.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT a. “Xu hua” 敘畫 (Explaining painting)
2 CONT
2 CONT Texts and Commentaries
2 CONT
2 CONT * Chen Chuanxi 陳傳席, trans. and comm. Wu Zhuo 吳焯, coll. Xu Hua 敘畫. Beijing: Renmin meishu chubanshe, 1985. 
2 CONT * Chen Chuanxi 陳傳席, ed. Liuchao hualun yanjiu 六朝畫論研究, 143–78. Taipei: taiwan xuesheng shuju, 1991. 
2 CONT * Okamura shigeru 岡村繁. Yu Weigang 俞慰剛, trans. Lidai minghua ji yizhu 歷代名畫記譯注, 328–32. Gangcun Fan quanji 岡村繁全集, Vol. 6. Shanghai: Shanghai guji chubanshe, 2002.
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * He Chuxiong 何楚熊. “Wang Wei yu ‘Xu hua' ” 王微與《敘畫》. Wenyi lilun yu piping 2 (1995): 98–103. 
2 CONT * Chen Chuanxi 陳傳席, ed. Liuchao hualun yanjiu 六朝畫論研究, 165–77. Taipei: Taiwan xuesheng shuju, 1991.
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhonggu wenxue shiliao congkao, 282, 322. 
2 CONT * Tian Li 田力. “Chongjue Wang Wei ‘Xu hua' de meixue jiazhi” 重掘王微《敘畫》的美學價值. Yishu tansuo 18.4 (2004): 39–40. 
2 CONT * Bush, Susan. “The Essay on Painting by Wang Wei 王微 (415–453) in Context.” in Chinese Aesthetics: The Ordering of Literature, the Arts, and the Universe in the Six Dynasties, ed. Zong-qi Cai, 60–80. Honolulu: University of Hawaii
2 CONC  Press, 2004.
2 CONT
2 CONT Translations
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sakanishi, Shio. The Spirit of the Brush, 42–45. 1939; rpt. London: John Murray, 1957. 
2 CONT * Sirén, Osvald. The Chinese on the Art of Painting, 17–18. 1936; rpt. Hong Kong: Hong Kong University Press, 1963. 
2 CONT * Acker, Some T'ang and Pre-T'ang Texts on Chinese Painting, 2: 130–31. 
2 CONT * Delahaye, Hubert. Les premiers peintures de paysage en Chine: aspects religieux. Paris: Ecoles française d'Extrême orient, 1981. 
2 CONT * Bush, Susan and Hsio-yen Shih, ed. Early Chinese Texts on Painting, 38–39. Cambridge: Harvard University Press, 1985.
2 CONT
2 CONT b. Poems
2 CONT
2 CONT Translations
2 CONT
