1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Zhong Yan 鍾琰 (fl. 260). Western Jin female poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zhong Yan's ancestral home was Changshe 長社 in Yingchuan 穎川 (northeast of modern Changge 長葛, Henan). She was the great-granddaughter of Zhong You 鍾繇 (ca. 163–230) and the daughter of Zhong Hui 鍾徽. She was the wife of Wang Hun 王渾 (2
2 CONC 23–297) and the mother of Wang Hun's second son Wang Ji 王濟 (ca. 240–ca. 285). When only a few years of age, Zhong Yan reputedly was able to write. As she grew older, she had read a wide range of texts. She was also a skilled singe
2 CONC r. The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists a collection of her writings in five ''juan''. The two Tang histories record a collection in two ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Fragments of two ''fu'', “Xisi fu” 遐思賦
2 CONC  (''Fu'' on distant longings) and “Ying fu” 鶯賦 (''Fu'' on the oriole), have been preserved in Yan Kejun's ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''. Lu Qinli records two lines of a tetrasyallbic poem attributed to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 308.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
