1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Xie Zhuang''' 謝莊 (421–466), ''zi'' '''Xiyi''' 希逸. Liu-Song period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Zhuang's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 (modern Taikang, Henan). Xie Zhuang's father Xie Mi 謝密, better known by his ''zi'' Hongwei 弘微 (392–433), was a high official during the early Liu-Song period. Xie Zhuang was a precocious 
2 CONC child and reputedly could compose at the age of seven. His father died in 433 when Xie Zhuang was twelve. Ca. 440, Xie Zhuang was assigned as an aide to Liu Jun 劉濬 (429–453), Prince of Shixing, the second son of Emperor Wen (r. 42
2 CONC 4–453). A few years later, he was appointed instructor to Emperor Wen's fifth son, Liu Shao1 劉紹 (432–452), Prince of Luling. He also served as secretary and librarian in the household of Emperor Wen's eldest son, the crown prince 
2 CONC Liu Shao2 劉劭 (425–453). During his service with Liu Shao1, Xie Zhuang resided in Jiangzhou 江州 (administrative seat Chaisang 柴桑, southwest of modern Jiujiang 九江, Jiangxi). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ca. 449 Xie Zhuang joined the staff of Liu Dan 劉誕 (433–459), Prince of Sui, who had just been appointed regional inspector of Yongzhou 雍州 (administrative seat, Xiangyang). Xie Zhuang was an important member of Liu Dan's staff in X
2 CONC iangyang. He also did some scholarly work including preparing a version of the ''Zuo zhuan'' that divided the text into accounts relating to each individual state of the Chunqiu period. He also constructed a ten-foot-square wooden
2 CONC  map of the realm on which mountains, water-ways, and contours of the land were clearly represented. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 452, Emperor Wen summoned Xie Zhuang to the captial where he was appointed palace cadet for the crown prince Liu Shao2. Upon imperial command he composed a fu on a beautiful red parrot that was presented to the court. Xie's “''
2 CONC Fu'' on the Scarlet Parrot” received special praise from Yuan Shu 袁淑 (408–453), a leading scholar and poet of the time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 453 Liu Shao2, known posthumously as the Arch-fiend 元凶,  assassinated his father Emperor Wen and declared himself emperor. Emperor Wen's third son, Liu Jun 劉駿 (the future Emperor Xiaowen 孝文,  r. 454–464), led an army agai
2 CONC nst him. He entrusted Xie Zhuang with correcting and polishing the war proclamations issued against Liu Shao2. When Liu Jun assumed the throne in May of 453, he appointed Xie Zhuang palace attendant. In 454, Xie Zhuang was promote
2 CONC d to left general of the guards and minister of the Ministry of Personnel. However, because Xie was suffering from a very painful illness, which he describes in a letter to the Prince of Jiangxia 江夏, Liu Yigong 劉義恭 (403–465), he r
2 CONC esigned from the Ministry of Personnel in 456. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Zhuang returned to office in 457 in the position of minister of the section for justice. In 458, as a result of changes in the administrative structure, Xie was appointed head of the Ministry of Personnel, a position he held j
2 CONC ointly with Gu Jizhi 顧覬之 (392–467). Soon thereafter Xie was promoted to right general of the guards as well as supervising secretary. Upon imperial command, he composed a fu and song in praise of a dancing horse that had been pres
2 CONC ented to the court from Henan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 461, Xie Zhuang returned to his former position of palace attendant and was put in command of the General of the Forward Army. In 462, he again was head of the Ministry of Personnel. He also was assigned as professor at the nat
2 CONC ional university. In the same year he composed “Song Xiaowu Xuan guifei lei” 宋孝武宣貴妃誄 (Dirge for Emperor Xiaowu's Honorable Consort Xuan”) and a lament to mourn the passing of Emperor Xiaowu's concubine Yin Shuyi 殷淑儀. The emperor r
2 CONC eputedly was moved to tears when he read the lament. So many people in the capital wished to copy it, the price of paper and ink reputedly rose. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 463, the son of Yan Shibo 顏師伯 (419–465) recommended a commoner for the post of magistrate of official carriages. Even after the emperor judged that the man's status was inappropriate for this postion, the officials in charge al
2 CONC lowed him to assume the post. When their failure to follow the emperor's edict was discovered, numerous officials were punished. Xie Zhuang, as head of the Ministry of Personnel, was removed from office. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Zhuang subsequently joined the staff of Liu Ziluan 劉子鸞 (d. 465), Prince of Xin'an 新安, as senior administrator, in which post he served until the accession of Liu Ziye 劉子業 (posthumously known as the Former Deposed Emperor, r. 4
2 CONC 64–65) as emperor in July 464, when he was recalled to the capital where he was granted the honoric title grand master of the palace with golden seal and purple ribbon. Xie also was commissioned to compose the official lament for 
2 CONC the deceased Emperor Xiaowu (r. 464–465). However, Liu Ziye long had resented Xie Zhuang, for he was deeply offended by the dirge Xie had written for the Honorable Consort Xuan, which contained a line he considered derogatory to h
2 CONC im and his late mother. Although he first proposed having Xie Zhuang put to death, he was persuaded to punish him by assigning him to the Directorate of Imperial Manufactories, where Xie presumably was given the menial task of mak
2 CONC ing utensils and tools. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 January 466, Liu Ziye was assassinated and replaced by the eleventh son of Emperor Wen, Liu Yu 劉彧 (posthumously known as Emperor Ming 明, r. 466–472). Xie Zhuang was immediately recalled to office and appointed cavalier attend
2 CONC ant-in-ordinary, household grandee, and put in charge of the army of the Prince of Xunyang 尋陽. He also composed the edict that announced a general amnesty as well hymns in praise of Emperor Xiaowu. Before he died in 466 at the age
2 CONC  of forty-six, Xie Zhuang held the positions of secretariat director, cavalier attendant-in-ordinary. He also had an official retinue of twenty persons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Zhuang's collected works originally was quite large: nineteen ''juan'' and over four hundred works of verse, ''fu'', and prose. The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists his collected works in nineteen ''juan''. I
2 CONC t also mentions a Liang dynasty catalogue that recorded a version in fifteen ''juan''. Both Tang histories also list a collection in fifteen ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Later collections are reconstructions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of Xie Zhuang's compositions were pieces written for court occasions. For example, he wrote a ''fu'' on a team of dancing horses that was presented to the emperor. Xie Zhuang's best-known work is “Yue fu” 月賦 (''Fu'' on the mo
2 CONC on). He also has a set of zayan 雜言 (irregular length line) poems that are written in simple language. He is acknowledged as the first poet to write in this form. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Zhuang was also a painter and an expert on music. He reputedly formulated some prosodic rules that were precursors to the tonal theories of the Yongming period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Collections
2 CONT
2 CONT * Zhang Xie 張燮 (1574–1640), ed. Xie Guanglu ji 謝光祿集. 3 juan + 1 juan supplement. Qishier jia ji 七十二家集. 
