1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sima Biao 司馬彪 (240–ca. 306; alt. 241?–305? alt. ca. 247–306), ''zi'' Shaotong 紹統. Wei and Jin period historian and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sima Biao's ancestral home was Wen 溫 in Henei 河內 (west of modern Wen county, Henan). He was the eldest son of Sima Mu 司馬睦 (d. 291?), Prince of Gaoyang 高陽. Sima Biao was adopted by his father's uncle Sima Min 司馬敏 (n.d.), the younge
2 CONC st brother of Sima Yi 司馬懿 (179–251), who was posthumously honored as Emperor Xuanwen 宣文 of the Jin dynasty. Sima Biao was a bright child, and from an early age he was a diligent student. However, he was something of a flirt and ph
2 CONC ilanderer, and this is the reason why he was disinherited by his father and sent to live with his uncle. From this point, Sima Biao withdrew from society and concentrated on study. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 261, Shan Tao 山濤 (205–283) was serving as director of the Ministry of Personnel. Sima Biao wished to impress him with the hope of getting Shan's recommendation for an official post. He sent a poem to Shan Tao, who used his infl
2 CONC uence to arrange Biao to be appointed commandant of cavalry. Ca. 266, he was appointed assistant and then vice director of the palace library. During his time he wrote a commentary to the ''Zhuangzi'' 莊子 and compiled the ''Jiuzhou
2 CONC  chunqiu'' 九州春秋 (Annals of the nine regions). The latter was an historical account in ten ''juan'' of the last years of the Later Han dynasty. it was written in the style of the ''Guo yu'' 國語. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sima Biao also wrote during this period the ''Xu Han shu'' 續漢書 (sequel to the history of the Later Han), which is an eighty-''juan'' history of the entire Eastern Han period, from Emperor Guangwu 光武 (r. 25–57) to Emperor Xian 獻 (r
2 CONC . 189–220). It had three divisions of ''ji'' 紀 (annals), ''zhi'' 志 (monographs) and ''zhuan'' 傳 (accounts). 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the early Taishi period (265–274), Emperor Wu personally took charge of the southern suburban sacrifices. After verifying the details of the sacrificial ceremony, Sima Biao presented the “Jiaosi zhi” 郊祀志 (Monograph on the s
2 CONC uburban sacrifices) that he compiled for the ''Xu Han shu'' to Emperor Wu. Ca. 294 Sima Biao was appointed gentleman cavalier attendant. He died around 305 at the age of sixty-plus years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier in the Western Jin, Qiao Zhou 譙周 (200–270) had written the ''Gu shi kao'' 古史考 (investigations of ancient history) in twenty-five ''juan'', which largely is a critical commentary to Sima Qian's 司馬遷 ''Shi ji'' 史記. Sima Biao,
2 CONC  based on the ''Jizhong jinian'' 汲冢紀年 (Annals from the Ji tumulus), added commentary on 122 entries in the ''Gu shi kao''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of Sima Biao's writings have been lost. During the Liang dynasty Liu Zhao 劉昭 (fl. 502–519) incorporated Sima Biao's eight monographs in his ''Hou Han shu'' 後漢書 (History of the Later Han). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Sima Biao's collected works in four ''juan'', and mentions a Liang period catalogue that recorded a collection in three ''juan''. Both Tang histories list a collection in thre
2 CONC e ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Yan Kejun's ''Quan shangguo Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen'' contains four of Sima Biao's prose writings: “Bo si liu zong biao” 駁祀六宗表 (Petition rebutting the proposal to offer sacrifice to
2 CONC  the six Venerable Ones), “Yu shan Juyuan shu” 與山巨源書 (Letter to Shan Juyuan), “Xu Han shu xu” 續漢書敘 (Autobiographical postface to the ''Xu Han shu''), “Xu Han shu xu Guangwu ji lun” 續漢書光武紀論 (Disquistion on the “Annals of Guangwu” i
2 CONC n the ''Xu Han shu'') and “He di ji lun” 和帝紀論 (Disquistion on the “Annals of Emperor He”). His extant poems include the two-part “Zeng Shan Tao shi” 贈山濤詩 (Poem to Shan Tao) and a fragmentary untitled poem, which Lu Qinli has colle
2 CONC cted in the ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbeichao shi''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 89. 
2 CONT * Mansvelt Beck, B. J. The Treatises of Later Han: Their Author, Sources, Contents and Place in Chinese Historiography, 8–12, 97–103. Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1990. 
2 CONT * Song Zhiying 宋之英. “Sima Biao Xu Han shu kaobian” 司馬彪《續漢書》考辨. Shixue shi yanjiu 118 (2005): 25–32. 
2 CONT * Luo Yanmin 羅彥民. “Sima Biao Zhuangzi zhu kaolun” 司馬彪《莊子》注考論. Wuyi daxue xuebao (shehui kexue ban) 13.1 (2011): 32–35. 
2 CONT * Li Yiting 李毅婷. “Xi Jin qianqi zhengzhi sixiang di xuanxuehua—yi Sima Biao wei zhongxin” 西晉前期政治思想的玄學化—以司馬彪為中心. Dongyue luncong 33.2 (2012): 65–71.
2 CONT
