1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Fei 謝朏 (441–506/507), ''zi'' Jingchong 敬冲. Song, Qi and Liang period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Fei's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). His grandfather was Xie Hongwei 謝弘微 (392– 433). His father Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421–466) had five sons. Xie Fei was the second son. Xie Fei was abl
2 CONC e to compose prose and verse at the age of ten. Xie Zhuang wrote a poem at Tushan 土山 (southeast of modern Nanjing). He asked Xie Fei to write a matching piece. Xie took up his brush and dashed off a poem. Wang Yu 王彧 (413–472) call
2 CONC ed him a “child prodigy.” Xie Hongwei replied that Fei “truly is the thousand pieces of gold in our family.” When Emperor Xiaowu (r. 453–464) traveled to Gushu 姑孰 (modern Dangtu 當涂, Anhui), he ordered Xie Zhuang to bring Xie Fei a
2 CONC long in the imperial entourage. The emperor commanded Fei to compose an encomium on a deep hole. Fei composed the piece on the spot. The emperor exclaimed, “Although he is small, he is an unusual lad.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Fei began his official career as acting administrator in the judicial section of the administration of the General of the Pacification Army. He later was appointed secretary to the heir designate Liu Ziye 劉子業 (449–465). In 466
2 CONC , Xie Fei resigned from office to observe mourning for his father Xie Zhuang. After the mourning period, he returned to the court where he was named director in the Department of State Affairs. He also served as senior administrat
2 CONC or on the staff of the regent Yuan Can 袁粲 (420–478). He was sent out as administrator in Linchuan 臨川 (west of modern Fuzhou 撫州, Jiangxi). Someone charged him with accepting bribes and escaped punishment only after Yuan Can came to
2 CONC  his defense. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 477, Xiao Daocheng 蕭道成 (427–482), the future founder of the Southern Qi dynasty, had taken control of the government and installed a puppet emperor, Liu Zhun 劉準 (469–479), on the throne. Xiao Daocheng appointed Xie Fei to his s
2 CONC taff as senior administrator. Xie Fei along with Chu Xuan 褚炫 (fl. 477–485), Jiang Xiao 江斅 (452–495), and Liu Yu 劉俣 (d. 477) served as personal attendants to the emperor. They were known as the “Four Companions of the Son of Heaven
2 CONC .” When Xiao Daocheng was planning to have Emperor Shun abdicate the throne, he discussed the precedents of the Wei and Jin period with Xie Fei, whom he respected for his distinguished family background. Xie Fei not so subtlety ad
2 CONC vised him not to follow this course. Displeased with Xie's reply, Xiao transferred him to the position of palace attendant and put him in charge of the palace library. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Xiao Daocheng was installed as the first emperor of the Qi dynasty, according to protocol the palace attendant was expected to remove his seals and present them to the new emperor. Because Xie Fei refused to do so, Xiao Daoc
2 CONC heng had him removed from office and sent home. Xie Fei returned to official service in 483 as senior recorder for comprehensive duty. He was soon assigned to his former post of palace attendant and named professor at the national
2 CONC  university. In 487, Xie Fei was sent out as governor of Yixing 義興 (administrative seat Yangxian 陽羨, modern Yi xing, Jiangsu). During his tenure in this position, Xie Fei made a point of not attending to administrative affairs. In
2 CONC  490, he returned to the imperial court where he served as minister of the section for justice of the Department of State Affairs and secretariat director. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Fei became increasingly concerned about the perils service at the imperial court posed for him and his family. In 494 he requested to be assigned to a provincial post. He was granted the position of governor of Wuxing 吳興 (admi
2 CONC nistrative seat Wucheng 烏程, south of modern Huzhou 湖州, Zhejiang). Xie Fei spent most of his time not on official duties but discussing literature and history with the scholar Zhou Xingsi 周興嗣  (d. 521). In 497, the emperor wished t
2 CONC o summon Xie Fei back to the court, but he remained in Wuxing caring for his mother. He refused a similar invitation in 500. In December 501, Xiao Yan 蕭衍 (464–549), the future founder of the Liang dynasty, led an army into the cap
2 CONC ital and deposed Xiao Baojuan 蕭寶卷 (483–501), the penultimate Southern Qi ruler. Xiao Yan had de facto control of the court. He summoned Xie Fei to come to the capital, but he refused the invitation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Xiao Yan was installed as emperor in April 502, he issued an invitation to Xie Fei to assume the position of palace attendant. Again, Xie Fei declined. In the sixth lunar month of 503, Xie Fei finally consented to go to the c
2 CONC apital, where he was appointed palace attendant, minister of education, and director of the Imperial Secretariat. However, he did not pay his respects to the emperor on the pretext that he was hampered by extreme pain in his legs.
2 CONC  Xiao Yan arranged for a reception in the Hualin Park where he was allowed to greet the emperor in a small cart. The next day, Xiao Yan personally visited Xie Fei at Fei's residence. Soon thereafter Xie Fei requested to return to 
2 CONC Wuxing to bring his mother to the capital. Xiao Yan hosted a farewell banquet for him and presented him a poem. He also had a special villa built for him in the capital. He also excused him from the requirement of reporting to the
2 CONC  court on the first and fifteenth days of the month. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first lunar month of 506, Xie Fei was appointed secretariat supervisor, minister of education, and general of the guards. He died of illness in the twelfth lunar month of Tianjian 5. Thus, it is likely that he died in 507 r
2 CONC ather than 506. Xie Fei was about sixty-six years old at this time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Fei was a skilled poet from an early age. Xiao Daocheng once asked the famous statesman and scholar Wang Jian 王儉 (452–489) who in the present age was skilled at composing pentasyllabic verse. Wang replied, “Xie Fei has attaine
2 CONC d his father's rich fertility.” The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Xie Fei's collected works in fifteen ''juan''. This was lost in the Tang. All that survives of his writings are fragments of two letters, one t
2 CONC o Wang Jian, and another to Xie Fei's younger brother Xie Yue 謝𤅢 (454–498). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 450–51. 
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. Dong Jin Nanchao Xieshi wenxue jituan 東晉南朝謝氏文學集團, 255–58. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998.
2 CONT
