1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Hao 謝顥 (d. ca. 493), ''zi'' Renyou 仁悠. Song and Southern Qi period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Hao's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 (modern Taikang, Henan). He was the third son of Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421–466). His birth and death dates are not known. During his youth, he was frugal and calm, and had a dignified and refined d
2 CONC emeanor. His first official position was administrator in the palace library. At the end of the Song, he served on the staff of Xiao Ze 蕭賾 (427–482), the future Emperor Gao of the Southern Qi (r. 482–493). He also served as govern
2 CONC or of Yuzhang 豫章 (modern Nanchang, Jiangxi). He was dimissed from office for climbing the beacon tower at the Shitou 石頭 fortress while wearing a white uniform. In the early Southern Qi, Xie Hao was assigned as companion to the Pri
2 CONC nce of Jingling, Xiao Ziliang (460–494). He also served as director of the Ministry of Personnel. He died ca. 493 while serving as a senior administrator on the staff of the Prince of Baling, Xiao Zilun 蕭子倫 (468–494). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists his collected works in sixteen ''juan''. This was lost already in the Tang. None of his writings has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 458.
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. Dong Jin Nanchao de Xie shi wenxue jituan, 258–59. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998.
2 CONT
