1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Yue 謝𤅢 (454–498), ''zi'' Yijie 義潔. Southern Qi literatus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Yue's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). His grandfather was Xie Hongwei 謝弘微 (392– 433). His father Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421–466) had five sons. Xie Yue was his youngest son. His brother X
2 CONC ie Fei 謝朏 (441–506/507) was a prominent literary figure of the Southern Qi and early Liang. When Xie Yue was seven years old, Wang Yu 王彧 (413–472) praised him to Emperor Xiaowu (r. 453–464), who was so impressed with Yue's relaxed
2 CONC  and graceful bearing and skill at repartée, he wished to have him married to one of his daughters. However, after Emperor Xiaowu died and was briefly succeeded by his son Liu Ziye 劉子業 (449–465) only to be killed by Liu Yu 劉彧 (439
2 CONC –472), the marriage arrangements were never completed. Xie Yue then married a daughter of Chu Yuan 褚淵 (435–482). 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Shengming period (477–479) of the Song, Xie Yue began his official career as an aide to Shen Youzhi 沈攸之 (d. 478), to be followed by an appointment as assistant in the palace library. He later served as an aide to the Mi
2 CONC nister of Education Yuan Can 袁粲 (420–478) and governor of Danyang 丹陽 (the area that administered the capital area). He subsequently was assigned to the labor section in the administration of Liu Xie 劉燮 (n.d.), Prince of Jinxi. In 
2 CONC 479, he served as an administrator on the staff of Xiao Ze 蕭賾 (440–493), the eldest son of Xiao Daocheng 蕭道成 (427–482), the future founder of the Southern Qi dynasty. In May 479, Xiao Daocheng assigned Xie Yue secretary in Xiao Ze
2 CONC 's administration. In that same month, Xiao Daocheng assumed the throne as emperor of the Southern Qi. He appointed Xie Yue companion to his eighth son Xiao Shuo 蕭鑠 (470–494), Prince of Guiyang. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Feeling obliged to care for his aged mother, Xie Yue requested to be assigned to a provincial post. He was appointed administrator of Ancheng 安成 (administrative seat Pingdu 平都, southeast of modern Anfu 安福, Jiangxi). He returned to
2 CONC  the capital as a senior administrator to the promi-nent statesman and scholar Wang Jian 王儉 (452–489). He thereafter held a succession of court posts and served a term as governor of Wuxing 吳興 (administrative seat Wucheng 烏程, sout
2 CONC h of modern Huzhou 湖州, Zhejiang). While in Wuxing, he received a visit from his elder brother Xie Fei. At a parting banquet, Xie Fei pointed to Yue's mouth and said, “All that this is fitting for is drinking alcohol.” This was a w
2 CONC arning to be circumspect in his speech in the precarious political situation that prevailed at that time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Yue had to resign his position to observe mourning for his mother. He returned to the imperial court as minister of the Ministry of Personnel. When in September 494 Xiao Luan 蕭鸞 (452–498) deposed and killed the young ruler, th
2 CONC e “Prince of Yulin,” guards and attendants rushed to report the matter to Xie Yue. Yue was in the middle of playing a game of ''weiqi'' (Encirlement chess). Each time he moved a piece, people said “He must have some intent in mind
2 CONC .” After the game was completed, Xie returned home and went to bed. From this time on he did not concern himself with government affairs. When Xiao Luan had himself installed as emperor in December 494, Xie Yue, on grounds of illn
2 CONC ess, requested not to oversee administative matters. However, he did hold several positions during Xiao Luan's rule including palace attendant, palace cadet and supervisor of the household of the heir designate, rectifier of Yuzho
2 CONC u, and cavalier attendant-in-ordinary. Xie Yue died of illness in 494 at the age of forty-five. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Yue was a skilled writer. During the Yongming era (483–493) of the Southern Qi when Xiao Ze ordered the construction of the Chanling Monastery, he commisioned Xie Yue to compose a stele inscription to commemorate the event. Th
2 CONC e monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Xie Yue's collected works in ten ''juan''. This was lost already in the Tang. None of his writings has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
