1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xiao Tui 蕭推 (d. 548), ''zi'' Zhijin 智進. Liang period noble and literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Tui's ancestral home was Nan Lanling 南蘭陵 (the Danyang 丹陽 area northwest of modern Changzhou, Jiangsu). He was the second son of Xiao Xiu 蕭秀 (475–518), Prince of Ancheng 安成, and a younger brother of Xiao Ji 蕭機 (499–528), a ren
2 CONC owned poet and writer in his day. None of his writings has survived. Xiao Tui was a precocious child. Xiao Gang  蕭綱 (503–551), Emperor Jianwen 簡文 (r. 549–551) greatly admired him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 525, as a common practice for imperial family member, he was named General Who Pacifies the Distance and governor of Huainan 淮南 (administrative headquarters in modern Shou 壽 county, Anhui). He later held many other posts, inclu
2 CONC ding general of light cavalry, governor of Jinling 晉陵 (administrative seat Zhenjiang 鎮江, southeast of modern Zhenjiang, Jiangsu), supervising secretary, frontrider of heir designate, vice director of the palace library, and govern
2 CONC or of Wu commandery 吳郡 (administrative seat Wu county 吳縣, modern Suzhou 蘇州, Jiangsu). During the Hou Jing rebellion of 548–552, Xiao Tui was dispatched to defend the Dongfu cheng 東府城 in the capital Jiankang. When the city fell int
2 CONC o the hands of Hou Jing's troops, Xiao Tui was killed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Tui's only extant poem, “Fu de cuishi ying ling shi” 賦得翠石應令詩 (In response to a command, a poem on a turquoise stone), is preserved in the ''Chuxue ji'' and Lu Qinli's ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin  Nanbeichao shi''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 394.
2 CONT
2 CONT TPK
