1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Xie Lan''' 謝覽 (ca. 477–ca. 513), ''zi'' '''Jingdi''' 景滌. Southern Qi and Liang period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Lan's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He is the grandson of Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421– 466) and the eldest son of Xie Yue 謝𤅢 (454–498). His uncle was Xie Fei 謝朏 (441–506/507), a prominen
2 CONC t literatus of the late Southern Qi and early Liang. Xie Lan and his younger brother Xie Ju 謝舉 (479–548) during their youth were already quite famous. There was a common saying: “The Wang family has Yang and Ju, and the Xie family
2 CONC  has Lan and Ju.” Yang 養 was the boyhood name of Wang Yun 王筠 (481–549), and Ju 炬 was the boyhood name of Wang Tai 王泰 (ca. 480–ca. 524). 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Southern Qi, Xie Lan married the Princess of Qiantang 錢唐. Xie Lan held the posts of commandant escort (a title conventionally assigned to imperial son-in-laws), assistant in the palace library, and secretary on the staf
2 CONC f of the crown prince. In 499, when Xiao Yan 蕭衍 (464–459), the future Emperor Wu of the Liang dynasty (r. 502–549), took control over the imperial court, he named Xie Lan master of ceremonies in the East Hall and administrator in 
2 CONC the revenue section of the counselor-in-chief (=Xiao Yan). 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Xiao Yan assumed the imperial throne in 502, he appointed Xie Lan vice director of the Secretariat, followed by an assignment as vice director of the Ministry of Personnel. Xie Lan had to leave office for several years to ob
2 CONC serve mourning for his mother. After the mourning period, he returned to the imperial court where he served as palace cadet on the staff of the heir designate Xiao Tong 蕭統 (501–531). He also had charge of affairs in the Ministry o
2 CONC f Personnel, was formally named director of the Ministry of Personnel, and then promoted to palace attendant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Lan was a heavy drinker, and at a banquet he insulted Xiao Chen 蕭琛  (ca. 465–531), for which offense he was impeached. However, Emperor Wu did not punish him on the grounds of his youth. He instead sent him out as senior admin
2 CONC istrator on the staff of the General of the Central Authority. Shortly thereafter he was recalled to the imperial court where he was assigned as secretary in the Eastern Palace of Xiao Tong. He subsequently served as governor of X
2 CONC in'an 新安 (administrative seat, Shixin 始新 (west of modern Chun'an 淳安, Zhejiang) where he accumulated a great amount of wealth. In 510, the “bandit” Wu Chengbo 吳承伯 (d. 510) rebelled at Xuancheng and led an attack on Xin'an. Xie Lan 
2 CONC fled to Guiji 會稽 (modern Shaoxing), returning to Xin'an only after the area was safe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Lan returned to the imperial court where he served as administrative adviser in the Ministry of Education. He was later appointed minister of the Ministry of Personnel, a post in which both his grandfather Xie Zhuang and fathe
2 CONC r Xie Yue had served. In 513, he was sent out as governor of Wuxing 吳興 (administrative seat Wucheng 烏程, south of modern Huzhou 湖州, Zhejiang). Unlike his service in Xin'an, Xie Lan did not engage in any activities to enrich himself
2 CONC . Xie Lan died in Wuxing at the age of thirty-seven. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Lan was a skilled poet, and at an imperial banquet Emperor Wu commanded Xie Lan and Wang Jian 王暕 (477–523) to compose poems matching his verses. He was extremely pleased with Xie Lan's composition, and requested him to write a
2 CONC  second poem, which exactly conformed to his sentiments. None of his writings survives.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
