1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Ju 謝舉 (479–548), ''zi'' Yanyang 言揚. Liang period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Ju's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He was the grandson of Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421– 466) and a son of Xie Yue 謝𤅢 (454–498). His uncle was Xie Fei 謝朏  (441–506/507), a prominent litera
2 CONC tus of the late Southern Qi and early Liang. Xie Ju and his older brother Xie Lan 謝覽 (ca. 477–ca. 513) during their youth were already quite famous. There was a common saying: “The Wang family has Yang and Ju, and the Xie family h
2 CONC as Lan and Ju.” Yang 養 was the boyhood name of Wang Yun 王筠 (481–549), and Ju 炬 was the boyhood name of Wang Tai 王泰 (ca. 480–ca. 524). The famous writer Jiang Yan 江淹 (444–505) remarked about the two Xie brothers: “this is what it m
2 CONC eans by ‘driving two dragons on a long road.'” When Xie Ju was fourteen, he presented a poem to Shen Yue 沈約 (441–513) who praised his composition.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early Liang, Xie Ju began his official career as assistant in the palace library. He was promoted to secretary on the staff of the crown prince Xiao Tong 蕭統 (501–531). He subsequently was assigned as vice director of the pa
2 CONC lace library, soon to be transferred as cadet and household provisioner on the staff of Xiao Tong. During this time Xie Ju was much admired by Xiao Tong. In 507, Ren Fang 任昉 (460–508) left the capital to take up a post as governor
2 CONC  of Xin'an 心安 (administrative seat, Shixin 始新, northwest of modern Chun'an 淳安, Zhejiang). Xie Ju composed a poem to send him off. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Once at a banquet at the Hualin Park, Emperor Wu asked Xie Lan about Xie Ju. Lan replied, “He is far beyond me in his knowledge of the Classics. It is only in drinking alcohol that he cannot compete with me.” Based on this informa
2 CONC tion, the emperor promoted Xie Ju to palace cadet and assigned him to oversee record keeping in Xiao Tong's Eastern Palace. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 512, Xie Ju was appointed palace attendant. In 515, he was sent out as an administrator in Yuzhang 豫章 (modern Nanchang, Jiangxi). He also held several military titles and served as governor of Jinling 晉陵 (admin-istrative seat, 
2 CONC modern Changzhou). When he left Jinling, the local officials requested to erect a stele in his honor. Xie Ju also held the distinction of serving as minister of the Ministry of Personnel three times (in 523, 525, and 528). In 533,
2 CONC  Xie Ju was assigned as right vice director of the Department of State Affairs, palace attendant, and put in charge of the court administration. In 537, he was sent out as General of the Cloud-like Flags and governor of Wu 吳 comma
2 CONC ndery (modern Suzhou). He later returned to the capital where he served as palace attendant and supervisor of the household of the crown prince Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503–551). In 543, he again served as vice director of the Department of 
2 CONC State Affairs. Although Xie held one of the highest posts in the imperial court, he showed little interest in administrative affairs. He often requested to resign from office on grounds of illness. Emperor Wu frequently granted hi
2 CONC m leaves and even gave him medicinal recipes that he had personally written. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 548, Xie Ju was appointed director of the Department of State Affairs. On 27 September 548, Hou Jing 侯景 (503–552) launched a rebellion against the Liang. By 7 December his army reached the outskirts of the capital Jiankang. Xie
2 CONC  Ju died in the siege of the Palace City. He had two sons, Xie Xi 謝禧 (n.d.) and Xie Gu 謝嘏 (510–569). 
2 CONT
2 CONT From his youth Xie Ju was broadly learned and a skilled writer of prose and verse. He also was well versed in arcane learning (xuan yan) and Buddhist philosophy. Ca. 507/508, he participated in the debates held at the Liang court 
2 CONC on the theory of the extinction of the soul espoused by Fan Zhen 范鎮 (ca. 450–ca. 510). When he was serving as governor of Jinling, he discussed various sutras with Buddhist monks. While in Wu commandery, he invited the recluse He 
2 CONC Yin 何胤 (446–531) to discuss the Mādhyamaka śāstra (Illumination of the middle way) with him. Xie and He exchanged poems, and Xie Ju composed a fu on the Huqiu Mountain Monastery 虎丘山 where He Yin resided. The piece, which was inscr
2 CONC ibed on a wall of the monastery, has been lost. Xie Ju also wrote a commentary on the Vimalakīrti nirdeśa sūtra which is also lost. According to Xie Ju's biography in the Nan shi, his collected works consisted of twenty juan. This
2 CONC  was lost before the Tang. Only one poem and one prose work (his essay on the extinction of the soul) are extant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
