1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Gu 謝嘏 (510–569), ''zi'' Hanmao 含茂 (also written Maofan 茂范). Liang and Chen period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Gu's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He was the second son of Xie Ju 謝舉 (479–548). His great-grandfather was Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421–466). During the Liang period he began his official
2 CONC  career as administrator in the palace library. In 547, he was assigned as palace cadet on the staff of the crown prince Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503–551). He also was put in charge of the records of the prince's Eastern Palace. He later was
2 CONC  sent out as governor of Jian'an 建安 (modern Jian'ou 建甌, Fujian). When the Hou Jing insurrection erupted in 548, Xie Gu fled to Guangzhou 廣州 (modern Guangdong) where he took refuge with Xiao Bo 蕭勃 (d. 557), a son of a first cousin 
2 CONC of Emperor Wu (r. 502–549), who was currently serving as regional inspector of Guangzhou. In 554, he was summoned to the imperial court to serve as minister of the Ministry of War but was unable to travel because the roads were im
2 CONC passable. Xiao Bo soon appointed Xie Zhen a senior administrator and assigned him as governor of Nanhai 南海 (modern Guangzhou). On 16 March 557, Xiao Bo rose “in revolt” against Chen Baxian 陳霸賢 (503–559), the future founding empero
2 CONC r of the Chen dynasty. Forces led by Zhou Wenyu 周文育  (509–559) and Hou Andu 侯安都 (520–563) put down the insurrection. Xiao Bo was killed by one of his officers on 29 April. Xie Gu went to Linchuan 臨川 (administrative seat, modern Fu
2 CONC zhou, Jiangxi), where he was detained by the local Linchuan satrap Zhou Di 周迪 (d. 565).
2 CONT
2 CONT After a few years, Xie Gu traveled to Jin'an 晉安 (administrative seat Houguan 侯官, modern Fuzhou, Fujian), where he joined Chen Baoying  陳寶應 (d. 564), the regional inspector of Minzhou (modern Fujian), who in 563 and 564 had rebelle
2 CONC d against Chen Baxian. Chen Baoying was defeated on 23 December 564. Xie Gu eventually was able to return to Jiankang where he was impeached by the palace aide to the censor-in-chief Jiang Dezao 江德藻 (509–565). Emperor Wen of Chen 
2 CONC (r. 559–566) decided not to punish him. He then granted him the supernumerary title of gentleman attendant at the palace gate. In 567, Xie Gu was appointed secretariat director, palace attendant, and minister of the Section for Ju
2 CONC stice of the Department of State Affairs. Xie Gu died in 569. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Xie Gu's biography in the ''Chen shu'', his collected works circulated in the early Tang. None of his writings has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 457. 
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. Dong Jin Nanchao Xieshi wenxue jituan 東晉南朝謝氏文學集團, 265. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
