2 DATE 483
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Chaozong 謝超宗 (d. 483). Liu-Song and Southern Qi literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Chaozong's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery. He was the grandson of Xie Lingyun 謝靈運 (385–433). Xie Chaozong's father Xie Feng 謝鳳 and Chaozong accompanied Xie Lingyun to his exile  in Guangzhou. After Xie Ling
2 CONC yun was executed in 433, Xie Feng remained in  Guangzhou where he died. Xie Chaozong returned north at the end of the Yuanjia period (424–454). Around this time he met the Buddhist monk poet Tang Huixiu 湯惠休 (d. post 466), with who
2 CONC m he shared a common interest in literature and scholarship. During the reign of the Liu-Song Emperor Xiaowu (r. 453–464), Xie Chaozong took up his first official post, audience attendant. In 461, Emperor Xiaowu (r. 453–464) grant
2 CONC ed his favorite son Liu Ziluan 劉子鸞 (456–465) the title Prince of Xin'an. He also appointed Xie Chaozong his attendant-in-ordinary. When Liu Ziluan's mother, Yin Shuyi 殷淑儀, died, Xie Chaozong composed the dirge. Upon reading the pi
2 CONC ece, Emperor Xiaowu exclaimed: “Xie Lingyun has come to life again.” Xie Chaozong was then appointed acting assistant to Liu Ziluan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Taishi period (465–471), Xie Chaozong served as assistant to Liu Xiuren 劉休人 (443–472), Prince of Jian'an, followed by an assignment at the imperial court as director of the palace in the Department of State Affairs. He was 
2 CONC later transferred to serve as administrator in the Ministry of Education. He subsequently was appointed assistant to the governor of Danyang 丹陽, which oversaw the administration of the capital district. Liu Xiuren also had Xie Cha
2 CONC ozong named record keeper in the Ministry of Education. Because he offended the vice director of the Department of State Affairs Liu Bing 劉秉 (433–477), he was demoted to senior recorder for comprehensive duty. Ca. 473, Xiao Daoche
2 CONC ng 蕭道成 (427–482) and Yuan Can 袁粲 (420–478) became impressed with Xie's literary skill, and they recommended his appointment as senior administrator to the general of the palace guard and governor of Linhuai 臨淮 (administrative seat
2 CONC , Xu 徐 county, northwest of modern Xutai 盱胎, Jiangsu). Ca. 477, he was assigned as governor of Yixing 義興 (administrative seat, modern Yixing, Jiangsu), but was dismissed for commiting an unspecified offense. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Xiao Daocheng began forming an army in opposition to the Liu-Song house, he appointed Xie Chaozong his adviser. After Xiao Daocheng established the Southern Qi dynasty in 479, he named Xie Chaozong gen-tleman of the palace ga
2 CONC te, and commisioned him to compose the lyrics for the suburban temple hymns. However, because of a violation of court etiquette, Xie Chaozong was dismissed from the court and sent out as aide to the crown prince Xiao Changmao 蕭長懋 
2 CONC (458–493). Because of excessive complaining about his treatment, he was dismissed from his post. In 480, Wang Sengqian 王僧虔 (426–485) departed the capital to take up his post as regional inspector of Xiangzhou 湘州 (administrative se
2 CONC at Linxiang 臨湘, modern Changsha). The Minister of Education Chu Yuan 褚淵 (435–482) saw him off. Just at that moment, the plank roadway broke apart and fell into the river. The oxen of the cart ridden by the vice director of the Dep
2 CONC artment of State Affairs Wang Jian 王儉 (452–489) shied, and he was thrown from his vehicle. Clapping his hands and laughing, Xie Chaozong remarked: “Falling into the water—one of the three excellencies; falling from the cart—the vi
2 CONC ce director.” His witticism became widely known both in and out of the court. However, Chu Yuan was much displeased by his remarks. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 482, when Emperor Wu (r. 482–493) acceded to the imperial throne, he put Xie Chaozong in charge of compiling the state history. He also appointed him adviser and secretary to the Prince of Jingling 竟陵, Xiao Ziliang 蕭子良 (460–494
2 CONC ). About this time Xie Chaozong arranged for his son to marry the daughter of Zhang Jing'er 張敬兒 (d. 483), a prominent general who had been a supporter of Xiao Daocheng before he became emperor. When Zhang Jing'er was executed, Xie
2 CONC  remarked to the governor of Danyang Li Anmin 李安民: “In years past they killed Han Xin, this year they kill Peng Yue. You governor—what do you plan to do?” Han Xin and Peng Yue were allies of Liu Bang, the founder of the Former Han
2 CONC . After the establishment of the Han, they both were executed. Li Anmin reported Xie Chaozong's remarks to Emperor Wu, who had him arrested. He was sentenced to exile in Yuezhou 越州 (administrative seat northwest of modern Hepu 河浦,
2 CONC  Guangxi). When Xie reached Yuzhang 豫章 (modern Nanchang), the emperor sent an edict commanding the adminisrator of Yuzhang to grant Chaozong the “privilege” of commiting suicide. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Chaozong was a skilled writer and learned scholar. During the reign of Emperor Xiaowu of the Song, when Xie was beginning his official career, he was commisioned to edit texts in the Eastern Palace of the crown prince with He 
2 CONC Fasheng 何法盛 (n.d.) and Shen Boyu 沈伯玉 (419–475). In 462, Xie Chaozong's great uncle Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421–466) composed a dirge for Emperor Xiaowu's favorite concubine, Yin Shuyi 殷淑儀. Xie Chaozong also composed a dirge that he present
2 CONC ed to the emperor, who was so impressed he exclaimed that “Chaozong very much has [his father] Feng's writing skill. I suspect that Xie Lingyun has come to life again.” When Xiao Daocheng ordered the composition of the suburban sa
2 CONC crificial hymns, he ordered Chu Yuan, Xie Fei 謝朏 (441–506), Kong Zhigui 孔稚珪  (447–501), Wang Xuanzhi 王咺之 (d. 502), Liu Rong 劉融 (n.d.), He Fajiong 何法冏 (n.d.), He Tanxiu 何曇秀 (n.d.), and Xie Chaozong to compose the lyrics. Only Chaoz
2 CONC ong's lyrics were selected. Zhong Rong ranks Xie Chaozong in the lower grade of his ''Shi pin''. He compared his verse to that of Yan Yanzhi 顏延之 (384–456). However, his only extant poems are the suburban temple hymns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies 
2 CONT
