1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Lin 謝藺 (510–547), ''zi'' Xiru 希如. Liang period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Lin's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He was the eighth-generation descendant of Xie An 謝安 (320–385). As a young boy Xie Lin was well known for his filial devotion to his parents.
2 CONC  At the age of five, he refused to eat until his parents had eaten. When his wet nurse urged him to eat first, he replied, “I do not feel hungry.” His maternal uncle Ruan Xiaoxu 阮孝緒 (479–536) was so moved, he compared him to Confu
2 CONC cius's disciple Zengzi, who was known for his filial piety, and Lin Xiangru 藺相如 (fl. 270–260 b.c.e.), the Warring States strategist famed for his devotion to his ruler. Thus, he was given the personal name Lin. After Xie Lin's fat
2 CONC her Xie Jing 謝經 died, Lin wailed day and night to the point that his body began to waste away. The Minister of the Ministry of Personnel Xiao Zixian 蕭子顯 (487–535) presented a petition to the imperial court praising him. Xie Lin wa
2 CONC s then appointed administrator in the law section of the princely establishment. He was later assigned as administrator in the Section for Outer Troops and secretarial aide in the princely establishment. After he presented a eulog
2 CONC y on sweet dew to the imperial court, Emperor Wu (r. 501–549) ordered him to compose a stele inscription in praise of the virtuous administration of Xiao Kai 蕭楷, regional inspector of Northern Yanzhou, and a eulogy for the Prince 
2 CONC of Xuancheng. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 547, Xie Lin was named gentleman cavalier attendant. That same year his mother passed away. Lin was so grief stricken, he began to spit up blood. He also refused to drink any liquids. After a month he died at the age of thirty-
2 CONC eight. According to Xie Lin's biography in the ''Liang shu'', he had written several tens of poems, ''fu'', stele inscriptions, and eulogies. None of his writings has survived. Xie Lin's son Xie Zhen 謝貞 (534–585) was a distinguish
2 CONC ed literatus of the Liang and Chen periods.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 456. 
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. Dong Jin Nanchao Xieshi wenxue jituan 東晉南朝謝氏文學集團, 265–66. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
