0 @N6000000002188195188@ NOTE Emperor Xianwen of Northern Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Bei) Wei Xianwendi ((?)????)
1 CONT Family name: Tuoba (??; tuò bá)
1 CONT Given name: Hong (?, hóng)
1 CONT Temple name: Xianzu (??, xian zu)
1 CONT Posthumous name: Xianwen (??, xiàn wén),
1 CONT literary meaning:
1 CONT "wise and civil"
1 CONT Tuoba Hong redirects here. For his son, the emperor who later changed the imperial clan name to Yuan, whose personal name is rendered the same way in pinyin, see Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xianwen of Northern Wei ((?)????) (454-476), personal name Tuoba Hong, was an emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. He was the first emperor in Chinese history who, after retiring in favor of his son Emperor
1 CONC  Xiaowen to become Taishang Huang (retired emperor) in 471, continued to hold onto power until his death in 476 -- when he was killed by his stepmother Empress Dowager Feng over his having executed a lover of hers.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Family background
1 CONT     * 2 Reign
1 CONT           o 2.1 Under Yifu Hun's regency
1 CONT           o 2.2 Under Empress Dowager Feng's regency
1 CONT           o 2.3 After assuming imperial authority
1 CONT     * 3 As retired emperor
1 CONT     * 4 Era names
1 CONT     * 5 Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family background
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Hong was born in 454, as Emperor Wencheng's oldest son. His mother was Consort Lin, who had previously been captured in war and had become a concubine of Tuoba Ren (???) the Prince of Yongchang, a distant relative of Emperor
1 CONC  Wencheng, who was executed in 453 after having been accused of crimes. After Tuoba Ren's death, she was seized and taken into the palace, where she became Emperor Wencheng's concubine. In 456, Emperor Wencheng created Tuoba Hong 
1 CONC crown prince -- and, at the same time, according to Northern Wei customs, ordered Consort Li to commit suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Under Yifu Hun's regency
1 CONT
1 CONT In 465, Emperor Wencheng died, and the 11-year-old Crown Prince Hong ascended the throne as Emperor Xianwen. Emperor Wencheng's wife Empress Feng was honored as empress dowager, and Emperor Xianwen's deceased mother Consort Li was
1 CONC  posthumously honored as Empress Yuan. Power soon fell into the hands of the official Yifu Hun, who assumed dictatorial powers and killed a large number of other officials, including Yang Baoping (???), Jia Airen (???) the Duke of
1 CONC  Pingyang, and Zhang Tiandu (???), Qiumuling Duohou (?????), Tuoba Yu (???) the Duke of Shunyang, and Emperor Wencheng's prime minister Buliugu Li. However, in 466, Empress Dowager Feng staged a coup and had Yifu arrested and exec
1 CONC uted. She assumed regency herself.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Under Empress Dowager Feng's regency
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Dowager Feng engaged Jia Xiu (??), Gao Yun, and Gao Lü (??) as her assistants in the regency. Later, she also incorporated her brother Feng Xi (??) into the decision-making circle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Dowager Feng was soon presented with a major opportunity to expand Northern Wei territory, as also in 466, rival Liu Song had a major dynastic succession struggle after Emperor Qianfei of Liu Song was assassinated in 465. 
1 CONC Emperor Qianfei's uncle Emperor Ming of Liu Song was declared emperor in the capital Jiankang, while his brother Liu Zixun the Prince of Jin'an was declared emperor in early 466 in Xunyang (??, in modern Jiujiang, Jiangxi). After 
1 CONC Emperor Ming's forces defeated Liu Zixun's and captured and executed Liu Zixun in fall 466, the Liu Song general Xue Andu (???), the governor of Xu Province (??, modern northern Jiangsu and northern Anhui), who had initially decla
1 CONC red for Liu Zixun, was apprehensive that he would be punished by Emperor Ming, and so decided to surrender to Northern Wei, and soon, he was joined by Bi Zhongjing (???) the governor of Yan Province (??, modern western Shandong) a
1 CONC nd Chang Zhenqi (???) the governor of Ru'nan Commandery (??, roughly modern Zhumadian, Henan). Empress Dowager Feng sent the general Weichi Yuan (???) to accept the surrenders of these Liu Song generals and to secure the region ju
1 CONC st north of the Huai River, and Weichi subsequently defeated two attempts by Emperor Ming to recapture those provinces. She also sent the general Murong Baiyao (????) to attack and try to capture Liu Song's Qing (??, modern centra
1 CONC l and eastern Shandong) and Ji (??, modern northwestern Shandong) Provinces, which were cut off from the rest of Liu Song after Xue's defection, and by 469, both provinces fell into Northern Wei hands, and all of the regions north
1 CONC  of the Huai River was by now Northern Wei territory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 467, Emperor Xianwen's concubine Consort Li -- a daughter of his uncle Li Hui (??) and therefore his cousin -- bore his oldest child Tuoba Hong (different character than his own), and Empress Dowager Feng personally raised the 
1 CONC young prince. She soon terminated her regency and returned imperial powers to Emperor Xianwen, who was 13 years old at this point.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] After assuming imperial authority
1 CONT
1 CONT As emperor, Emperor Xianwen was described as hardworking and appropriate in his awards and punishments -- in particular, in promoting honest officials and demoting corrupt ones, and it was said that only starting in his reign were
1 CONC  Northern Wei officials praised for being honest. He was also said to be heavily into studies of religions and philosophies, including both Taoism and Buddhism.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 469, Emperor Xianwen created Tuoba Hong crown prince. As Tuoba Hong's mother Consort Li died the same year and was described in traditional histories to be missed by all in the palace, she was probably forced to commit suicide 
1 CONC pursuant to Northern Wei customs.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 470, resentful that Murong Baiyao had flattered Yifu Hun while Yifu was in power, Emperor Xianwen, in an act that appeared to be uncharacteristic for his reign, falsely accused Murong Baiyao of treason and put him and his son M
1 CONC urong Ruyi (????) to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 470, an event would damage Emperor Xianwen's relationship with his stepmother Empress Dowager Feng. Empress Dowager Feng had taken the official Li Yi (??) as her lover. In 470, the official Li Xin (??), who was a close fri
1 CONC end of Li Yi's brother Li Fu (??), was accused of corruption, and Emperor Xianwen became aware of the accusations even though Li Fu tried to suppress the reports. He had known about his stepmother's relationship with Li Yi and, wh
1 CONC ile he had not taken any actions against it at that point, disapproved of it. He sented Li Xin to death, but then informed Li Xin that if he could report on crimes that Li Fu and Li Yi had committed, he would be spared. After init
1 CONC ial reluctance, Li Xin did so, and another man named Fan Biao (??) also did so. Emperor Xianwen then executed Li Fu and Li Xin. Empress Dowager Feng became resentful of Emperor Xianwen after that point.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 471, because of Emperor Xianwen's philosophical interests, he wanted to leave the throne to spend more time on his studies. He wanted to pass his throne to his uncle Tuoba Zitui (????) the Prince of Jingzhao, who was highly pra
1 CONC ised by all officials. He therefore summoned an imperial council to discuss the matter. The officials largely opposed Emperor Xianwen's proposal, and suggested instead that if Emperor Xianwen wanted to leave the throne, he should 
1 CONC pass the throne to Crown Prince Hong. He therefore did so, and the four-year-old crown prince took the throne as Emperor Xiaowen. Emperor Xianwen himself took the title of Taishang Huangdi (retired emperor).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As retired emperor
1 CONT
1 CONT As retired emperor, however, Emperor Xianwen continued to be in control of the imperial regime, and all important matters were still submitted to him. He spent much time on criminal law matters, and during this time, the criminal 
1 CONC cases generally were paid more attention, and while they took longer to process, the results were considered more just.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 476, still resentful of Emperor Xianwen, Empress Dowager Feng killed him. (Most historians, including Sima Guang, believed that she poisoned him, but another version indicated that Empress Dowager Feng readied assassins who, wh
1 CONC en Emperor Xianwen came to her palace to greet her, seized and smothered him.) She assumed regency over Emperor Xiaowen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Era names
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Tian'an (?? tian an) 466-467
1 CONT     * Huangxing (?? huáng xing) 467-471
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Emperor Wencheng of Northern Wei
1 CONT     * Mother
1 CONT           o Consort Li (forced to commit suicide 456), posthumously honored as Empress Yuan
1 CONT     * Major Concubines[1]
1 CONT           o Consort Li (d. 469[2]), mother of Prince Hong, daughter of Li Hui (??) the Prince of Nan Commandery, posthumously honored as Empress Si
1 CONT           o Consort Feng or Shiben, mother of Prince Xi
1 CONT           o Consort Han or Budahan, mother of Princes Gan and Yong
1 CONT           o Consort Meng, mother of Prince Yu
1 CONT           o Consort Poduolo or Pan, mother of Prince Xie
1 CONT           o Consort Gao, mother of Prince Xiang
1 CONT     * Children
1 CONT           o Tuoba Hong (???), the Crown Prince (created 469), later Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei
1 CONT           o Tuoba Xi (???), the Prince of Xianyang (created 485, forced to commit suicide 501)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Gan (???) (b. 469), initially the Prince of Henan (created 485, later Prince Ling of Zhao Commandery (created 494, d. 499)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Yu (???) (b. 470), Prince Hui of Guangling (created 485, died from injuries inflicted by lover's husband 501)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Yong (???), initially the Prince of Yingchuan (created 485), later Prince Wenmu of Gaoyang (created 494, killed by Erzhu Rong 528)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Xie (???), initially the Prince of Shiping (created 485), later Prince Wuxuan of Pengcheng (created 496, forced to commit suicide 508), posthumously honored as Emperor Wenmu
1 CONT           o Tuoba Xiang (???), Prince Ping of Beihai (created 485, d. 504)
1 CONT           o Princess Changshan
1 CONT           o Princess Leliang
1 CONT           o Princess Pengcheng
1 CONT           o Princess Le'an
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Despite the substantial length of his reign, Emperor Xianwen never created an empress. The reason is unknown.
1 CONT    2. ^ Consort Li was probably forced to commit suicide pursuant to Northern Wei customs as the mother of the crown prince, as it was that year that her son was created crown prince, but historical accounts are not conclusive on 
1 CONC the matter.
1 CONT
