0 @N6000000002188099869@ NOTE Emperor Ruizong of Tang
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Ruizong of Tang 
1 CONT Birth and death: June 22, 662–July 13, 716 
1 CONT Family name: Li (?), later Wu¹ (?),
1 CONT then later back to Li² (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Xulun³ (??), later Lun4 (?),
1 CONT then later Dan5 (?), then
1 CONT back to Lun6 (?), and
1 CONT eventually back to Dan7 (?) 
1 CONT Dates of reign (1st time): Feb. 27, 684–Oct. 19, 6908 
1 CONT Dates of reign (2nd time): July 25, 710–Sept. 8, 7129 
1 CONT Dynasty: Tang (?) 
1 CONT Temple name: Ruizong (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Never used short 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Emperor Xuanzhen Dasheng
1 CONT Daxing Xiao10
1 CONT ????????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar. 
1 CONT ——— 
1 CONT 1. In October 690, when he ceded the imperial throne to his mother
1 CONT Empress Wu and was made heir to the throne, his family name was
1 CONT changed into Wu which was the family name of his mother. 
1 CONT 2. His original family name Li, the family name of the emperors of
1 CONT the Tang Dynasty, was restored at a date not indicated by ancient
1 CONT Chinese historical books, perhaps in 705 at the fall of Empress Wu. 
1 CONT 3. This is the original given name of Emperor Ruizong. The literal
1 CONT meaning is "disk of the sun appearing above the horizon
1 CONT at daybreak". 
1 CONT 4. Had his name shortened into Lun ("disk") in December 669. 
1 CONT 5. Had his name changed into Dan (literal meaning: "dawn")
1 CONT in August 683. 
1 CONT 6. Had his name changed back to Lun when he ceded the imperial
1 CONT throne to his mother Empress Wu in October 690 and was made
1 CONT heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 7. Had his name changed back to Dan after he was reduced to a
1 CONT princely rank in 698 when his older brother Zhongzong replaced
1 CONT him as heir to the throne. This name became his taboo name when
1 CONT he was restored as emperor in 710. 
1 CONT 8. Ceded the imperial throne to his mother Empress Wu who
1 CONT became "emperor" while Ruizong was made heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 9. Abdicated in favor of his son Emperor Xuanzong and received
1 CONT the title Taishang Huang (???). In practice, continued to rule
1 CONT through his son until the palace coup of July 29, 713, and officially
1 CONT ceded full power to his son on July 30. 
1 CONT 10. Final version of his posthumous name as given in 754. 
1 CONT Emperor Ruizong (June 22, 662 – July 13, 716), personal name Li Dan, was the fifth and ninth emperor of Tang Dynasty. He was the eighth son of Emperor Gaozong and Empress Wu.
1 CONT
1 CONT In February 684, his mother Empress Wu demoted his older brother Emperor Zhongzong who had attempted to rule free of his mother, and named him emperor. Ruizong, however, was under control of his mother and didn't have any real pow
1 CONC er. In October 690 he ceded the imperial throne to his mother who was installed as "emperor" (huangdi, ??), the only woman in Chinese history ever to rule as emperor. Ruizong was reduced to the position of "heir to the throne". In
1 CONC  the following years, Empress Wu's family members (from the Wu family) tried to have one of them named heir to the throne, but the empress resisted these calls. Eventually, in October 698, faced with foreign invasion and dissatisf
1 CONC action at home, Empress Wu yielded to officials still loyal to the Li family (the Tang imperial family overthrown by the empress) and so she clearly hinted that she would be succeeded by a member of the Li family by appointing her
1 CONC  son the demoted Emperor Zhongzong as heir to the throne. On that occasion, Ruizong was reduced to a princely rank.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruizong lived the next twelve years away from political intrigues and did not take part in the coup that saw the overthrow of Empress Wu and the restoration of Zhongzong as emperor in 705. The five years of Zhongzong's reign were 
1 CONC dominated by Zhongzong's empress consort, Empress Wei (???), and her lover Wu Sansi (???), the nephew of the deceased Empress Wu, who both dominated the weak emperor. In the beginning of July 710, Emperor Zhongzong died, allegedly
1 CONC  poisoned by Empress Wei who then named Zhongzong's only surviving son, a teenager called Li Chongmao, as emperor. A mere two weeks later, Princess Taiping, the older sister of Ruizong, launched a coup with the help of Ruizong's t
1 CONC hird son, Li Longji (the future Emperor Xuanzong), which resulted in the death of Empress Wei and the overthrow of the teenage emperor. Ruizong was unwillingly restored as emperor on July 25 by his sister and his son. Li Longji, w
1 CONC ho had proved his worth during the coup, was named crown prince with the consent of Ruizong's eldest son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon, however, tensions between Princess Taiping and Li Longji mounted. Princess Taiping, who had many supporters at court, hoped to ascend the imperial throne as her mother Empress Wu had done. Ruizong exiled his sister to the pr
1 CONC ovinces, hoping to defuse tension, but Li Longji, who feared that Princess Taiping would hold him responsible for her exile and would have him murdered, asked his father to recall the princess, who was allowed to return to the imp
1 CONC erial court. Eventually, in September 712, a disgruntled Ruizong, tired of court feuds, abdicated in favor of his son the crown prince Li Longji. Suddenly it was Princess Taiping who was at risk of falling victim to her nephew the
1 CONC  new emperor. The princess, alarmed by Ruizong's decision, managed to have the court name him Taishang Huang (???, sometimes translated as "retired emperor", or "emperor emeritus"), a position in which Ruizong was to keep effectiv
1 CONC e control of power with the new emperor Xuangzong only formally attending court audiences and ceremonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fearing for her future, Princess Taiping launched a coup in the end of July 713 to overthrow Emperor Xuanzong. As she was a woman and could not personally lead troops, the coup failed and all her supporters were killed. Princess T
1 CONC aiping fled to a Buddhist monastery in the mountains. She returned three days later and was allowed to commit suicide at home. On July 30, one day after the coup, Ruizong officially announced he was ceding full power to his son Em
1 CONC peror Xuanzong. Ruizong was allowed to keep the title Taishang Huang but lived away from politics until his death three years later in 716.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Zhongzong Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 684–690 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Empress Wu 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Shang Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 710–712 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Emperor Xuanzong 
0 @N6000000002188101196@ NOTE Emperor Gaozu of Tang
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gaozu of Tang 
1 CONT Birth and death: 566–Jun. 25, 635 
1 CONT Family name: Li (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Yuan (?) 
1 CONT Courtesy name (?): Shude (??) 
1 CONT Dates of reign: Jun. 18, 618¹–Sept. 4, 626² 
1 CONT Dynasty: Tang (?) 
1 CONT Temple name: Gaozu (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Emperor Shenyao³ (????) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Emperor Shenyao Dasheng
1 CONT Daguang Xiao4
1 CONT ????????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar. 
1 CONT ——— 
1 CONT 1. Was already in control of Chang'an and de facto master of China
1 CONT since December 12, 617 
1 CONT 2. Abdicated in favor of his son, and was granted the title
1 CONT Taishang Huang (???), a title reserved for retired emperors. 
1 CONT 3. Given in 674 
1 CONT 4. Given in 754 
1 CONT Emperor Gaozu of Tang China (566 - June 25, 635), born Li Yuan, was the founder of the Tang Dynasty of China, and the first emperor of this dynasty from 618 to 626. Under the shortlived Sui dynasty, Li Yuan was the governor in the
1 CONC  area of modern-day Shanxi province, and was based in Taiyuan, Shanxi.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Establishment of the Tang Dynasty
1 CONT In 615, Li Yuan was assigned to garrison Lunghsi. He gained much experience by dealing with the Gokturks of the north and was able to pacify them. Li Yuan was also able to gather support from these successes and, with the disinter
1 CONC gration of the Sui dynasty in July of 617, Li Yuan - urged on by his second son Li Shimin (later Taizong emperor) - rose in rebellion. Using the title of "Great Chancellor" (???), Li Yuan installed a puppet child emperor, Emperor 
1 CONC Gong, but eventually removed him altogether and established the Tang Dynasty in 618.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign as Emperor
1 CONT Li Yuan's reign was concentrated on uniting the empire under the Tang. Aided by his son, Li Shimin, he defeated all the other contenders, including Li Gui, Dou Jiande, Wang Shichong, Xue Ju and Liu Wuzhou. By 628, the Tang Dynasty
1 CONC  had succeeded in uniting all of China. On the homefront, Li Yuan recognized the early successes forged by the Sui dynasty and strived to emulate most of its policies, including the equal distribution of land amongst its people, a
1 CONC nd he also lowered taxes. He abandoned the harsh system of law set about during the Sui dynasty as well as reforming the judicial system. These acts of reform paved the way for the reign of Emperor Taizong, which ultimately pushed
1 CONC  Tang China to the height of its power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Li Yuan abdicated in 626 in favour of his son, Taizong emperor after the Xuan Wu Gate incident (?????) when Taizong had to kill his two immoral brothers who were attempting to usurp the throne. Li Yuan lived on as "Grand Emperor" 
1 CONC (Taishang Huang) until his death in 635.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT (none) Emperor of the Tang Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Taizong 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Gong of Sui Emperor of China 
0 @N6000000002188193895@ NOTE Empress Yifu
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Yifu (????, personal name unknown) (510-540), formally Empress Wen (???, literally "the civil empress"), was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei state Western Wei -- a branch successor state of Northern Wei. Her husband was 
1 CONC Emperor Wen (Yuan Baoju).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 As empress
1 CONT     * 3 Death
1 CONT     * 4 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Her ancestors were ancestral chiefs of a branch tribe of Tuyuhun, and later became vassals of Northern Wei. After Northern Wei conquered Northern Liang in 439, her great-great-grandfather Yifu Mogui (????) led his tribe into North
1 CONC ern Wei and became a Northern Wei general. For three generations following his, the Yifus married Northern Wei princesses, and their daughters often married Northern Wei imperial princes. Her father Yifu Yuan (???) was a provincia
1 CONC l governor, and her mother was the Princess Huaiyang, a daughter of Emperor Xiaowen.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 525, when she was 15, she married Yuan Baoju, who was then 21 and a general under his cousin Emperor Xiaoming, but who carried no noble title because his father Yuan Yu (??) the Prince of Jingzhao had had his title stripped pos
1 CONC thumously after dying in a rebellion. In 535, after Northern Wei had split into Eastern Wei and Western Wei, he was made emperor (as Emperor Wen) by the general Yuwen Tai after the death of his cousin Emperor Xiaowu. He created he
1 CONC r empress and created her son Yuan Qin crown prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As empress
1 CONT
1 CONT As empress, Empress Yifu was said to be frugal, avoiding extravagant clothing, jewelry, and food, often eating just vegetables. She was also said to be kind and tolerant and never jealous, and Emperor Wen respected her greatly. Sh
1 CONC e bore him 12 children, although only Yuan Qin and Yuan Wu (??) the Prince of Wudu survived infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 538, with Western Wei occupied with wars against Eastern Wei and unable to fend itself against attacks by Rouran, Yuwen Tai wanted to further relationships with Rouran through an imperial marriage. He first had Emperor Wen crea
1 CONC te the daughter of the official Yuan Yi (??) as the Princess Huazheng and marry her to the Rouran Chiliantoubingdoufa Khan Yujiulü Anagui's brother Yujiulü Tahan (?????), and then further asked Emperor Wen to marry Yujiulü Anagui'
1 CONC s daughter. Emperor Wen was forced to agree. He deposed Empress Yifu and made her become a Buddhist nun, and he created Yujiulü Anagui's daughter empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Even though Empress Yifu had been deposed and made a nun, however, Empress Yujiulü was still jealous of her. To avoid conflict, in 540, Emperor Wen made Yuan Wu the governor of Qin Province (??, roughly modern Tianshui, Gansu), an
1 CONC d had Empress Yifu accompany Yuan Wu to his post. Emperor Wen, still harboring love for her in his heart, secretly requested that she start keeping her hair uncut so that he could eventually welcome her back to the palace. Unfortu
1 CONC nately, around the same time, Rouran launched a major attack against Western Wei, and a number of officials believed that the Rouran attack was made on behalf of Empress Yujiulü. Emperor Wen, with the officials pressuring him, sen
1 CONC t his eunuch Cao Chong (??) to Qin Province to order Empress Yifu to commit suicide. She made the comment to Cao:
1 CONT
1 CONT     May it be that His Imperial majesty live for a thousand years, and the empire is happy and calm. I do not have regrets about death.
1 CONT
1 CONT She then committed suicide, and was buried with honors due an empress, albeit not near the capital Chang'an, but instead at the Maiji Cliffs (???), near Shanggui (??) the capital of Qin Province. It was not until later, when Yuan 
1 CONC Qin was emperor, that she was reburied with Emperor Wen.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Gao Empress of Northern Wei (Western)
1 CONT 535-538 Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Yujiulü
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Zizhi Tongjian, vols. 157, 158.
1 CONT     * History of Northern Dynasties, vols. 5[1], 13[2].
0 @N6000000002188195798@ NOTE Empress Murong (Daowu)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Murong (????, personal name unknown), formally Empress Daowu (????), was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei Dynasty. Her husband was the founding emperor Emperor Daowu (Tuoba Gui).
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the youngest daughter of the Later Yan emperor Murong Bao (Emperor Huimin). When Northern Wei launched a major attack against Later Yan in 396, causing Murong Bao to abandon the capital Zhongshan (??, in modern Baoding, He
1 CONC bei) in 397, she was unable to follow her father in flight, and when Zhongshan fell to Northern Wei later that year, she was captured by Tuoba Gui and made one of his consorts. In 400, after he had declared imperial title, he cons
1 CONC idered whom to create empress. He had favored Consort Liu, the daughter of the Xiongnu chief Liu Toujuan (???) the most, and she also bore his oldest son Tuoba Si. However, the Tuoba Tribe's traditions dictated that when the ruler
1 CONC  were to consider whom to make his wife, he was to make the candidates try to forge golden statues by hand, as a way of discerning divine favor. Consort Liu was unable to forge a golden statue, while Consort Murong was able to, an
1 CONC d so was created empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nothing more is known about Empress Murong. She was implied to be still alive in 402, when Later Qin's emperor Yao Xing rejected Emperor Daowu's overture to marry one of his daughters, because Yao Xing's refusal was based on his i
1 CONC nformation that Emperor Daowu already had Empress Murong as empress.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT None (dynasty founded) Empress of Northern Wei
